From 4fc992f57cad271e63f568f1f0a1dc766cea5cbe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 06:08:19 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 097/105] kosmickrisp: complete the timeline signals a killed
 submission owed

Surviving a command queue failure is not enough on its own, because the
failure also destroys work the driver has already promised. A timeline is an
MTLSharedEvent and its signal is a queue-level operation ordered behind the
submission that precedes it, so a submission Metal kills takes its own signal
with it, and takes the signals of everything else it kills queued behind it.
Nothing raises those events afterwards.

The Vulkan fence therefore never signals and every waiter on it blocks
forever. For this driver that reaches past the process: a guest waiting on the
fence across the protocol stops too, and the host falls back on a fence escape
hatch that spends a fixed timeout per abandoned event. Twelve Vulkan CTS cases
that exceed an A15's command buffer limit produced nine reports and fourteen
abandoned fences, and one case whose fence no escape covered sat unfinished for
over ten minutes.

So the queue keeps the debt. Every signal it enqueues is recorded with the
serial of the submission it sits behind, and dropped again once the event
reaches that value, which a completion proves for everything ordered ahead of
it. A report then completes from the CPU whatever it destroyed: the signals at
or below the failing submission's serial for a failure the queue survived, and
all of them for one that lost the device, since a lost queue runs nothing
further.

Signals enqueued after the failing submission are left alone. That work is
still running, and raising its timeline early would tell a waiter its results
are ready while the GPU is still writing them; a failure that took those out as
well arrives as its own report and makes its own signals good. Records are
visited oldest first and skipped once the event has passed them, so a native
value that is set rather than advanced only ever moves up.

Carrying the serial needs the report to name one submission rather than the
queue, so the commit feedback payload becomes per-submission: it holds the
serial and a reference to the queue's shared feedback record, which already
outlives the Vulkan device for exactly this reason. The native event is
referenced rather than borrowed, because the vk_sync that enqueued a signal
can be destroyed before the signal comes due.

Two things still go missing after that. Metal reports a failure against one
submission, but the work it kills behind that submission does not always
arrive as a report of its own, so a signal enqueued after the reported one
can be destroyed with nothing naming it. A Vulkan CTS case on an A15 waited
on value 18 of a timeline whose event stopped at 16 with a signal for 18
already accepted, and the case never finished.

The queue resolves the first of those for free. It processes what it is given
in order, so a submission that completes also proves the queue went past every
signal enqueued before it; one of those still short of its value was discarded
rather than run. Once the queue has failed at least once, a completion
therefore completes them, and on a queue that has never failed nothing
changes.

The second needs a bound, because a queue that is not running anything reports
nothing at all, and then the only thing left that can notice is the caller
waiting on the value. It holds the evidence: the queue accepted a signal for
the value and has not delivered it. Waiting longer cannot help, since the
signal is not coming, so past ten seconds the wait completes what the queue
owes it and says so. That is two orders of magnitude over any submission that
runs to completion on the tightest device this driver targets, whose GPU stops
a command buffer after tens of milliseconds, and it costs nothing on a queue
that has never failed.

That bound is timed by the caller that wants the value, and only by one that
is waiting for the value to arrive. A caller asking whether the signal has
been submitted is answered from what the queue has enqueued and never reaches
the wait, and a caller that arrives wanting a different value starts its own
clock rather than inheriting one an earlier waiter left behind, so the elapsed
time always belongs to the value being recovered.

Waiting out that bound is the last resort and not the common case, because
Metal reports every submission it is given exactly once. When the number of
reports reaches the number of submissions the queue was handed, the queue is
holding nothing it has not answered for, and any signal still short of its
value has no submission left to deliver it, so it is completed there and then
rather than ten seconds later. Both the report and the submission check for
this, because a signal is enqueued after the submissions it follows and Metal
can report one of those before the commit call returns: the report that
empties the queue may run before the record it would have resolved exists, so
whichever of the two happens second does the work.

It matters at scale. A Vulkan CTS fraction on an A15 reported 776 timeouts in
its first hour, and the bound alone left 155 of them to be waited out, which
is 1550 of the 1877 seconds that hour spent past its first failure. A fraction
paying that is not going to finish.
